+# Summary
+
+## What changed
+- `ttlcache/cache.py` rewritten to meet `docs/CONTRACT.md`:
+  - Added the `clock=time.monotonic` parameter. Every time decision uses it. This was why all tests errored.
+  - One lock guards entries, in-flight loads and stats. It is never held while the loader runs.
+  - Per-key in-flight record with an `Event`. Concurrent `get` calls for a missing or expired key wait for the one load and share its value or the same exception object. This fixes the double load.
+  - Validity is `clock() - loaded_at < ttl`, so an entry exactly `ttl` old is expired. The old code used `<=`, which served expired values. `loaded_at` is read after the loader returns.
+  - Entries are checked by presence, not truthiness, so `None`, `0`, `""` and `False` are cached.
+  - A same-thread `get` for the key being loaded raises `RuntimeError`.
+  - Failures store nothing and the next `get` retries.
+  - `invalidate` removes only the stored entry and leaves any in-flight load alone.
+  - Stats are updated under the lock, so they are exact.
+  - There is no sleeping or polling.
+- Added `tests/test_contract.py` with new tests. No existing test was touched.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest`: 14 tests, OK. The 9 existing tests failed before the change.
+- Ran the full suite 40 times in a loop: 40 of 40 passed.
+
+## Not verified / caveats
+- Races are rare, so no test can prove their absence. The thread tests are few, and I did not stress test heavily.
+- Recursion is detected only on the same thread. A loader that hands the inner `get` to another thread and waits for it would deadlock. The contract doesn't cover that case.
+- A `BaseException` such as `KeyboardInterrupt` in a loader is also treated as a failure and re-raised to waiters.
+- `README.md` needs no change.
